2026-06-19 16:22:59,812 | AGENT | AIInnovation Agent received: The solution requires explainable recommendations or suggestions, with a posteriori understanding of elements leading to an output (source grounding, audit trail). It must be designed as an assistance tool, with no clinical or medico-administrative decision automated without explicit validation by a qualified professional. What AI components and innovation strategy can we propose to meet these requirements, and what are the known trade-offs?
2026-06-19 16:24:30,816 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/chat "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-06-19 16:24:30,816 | AGENT | AIInnovation Agent answered: Our company, Faber IA, can propose an AI solution that leverages retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) capabilities to provide explainable recommendations or suggestions. The RAG approach allows for source grounding and audit trails, enabling a posteriori understanding of elements leading to an output. This aligns with the requirement for explainability and transparency in the decision-making process.

To meet the constraint of not automating clinical or medico-administrative decisions without explicit validation by a qualified professional, our solution will be designed as an assistance tool. The AI system will propose recommendations or suggestions, but ultimate decision-making authority will remain with hospital staff. This approach ensures that the AI solution supports and augments human decision-making, rather than replacing it.

Our innovation strategy involves iteratively refining the RAG model through corpus selection and curation, retrieval tuning, prompt and template design, and evaluation loops. This iterative process allows us to adapt the AI solution to the specific needs and requirements of the hospital staff, while ensuring that the system remains transparent and explainable.

One known trade-off is the balance between performance and explainability. More complex AI models may offer higher performance but at the cost of reduced transparency and explainability. Our Sovereign European open-weight model, which is fully auditable and has strong grounding behavior, may be a suitable choice to address this trade-off. However, it may have somewhat lower raw performance on the most complex generative tasks compared to top-tier proprietary models.

Another trade-off is the need to balance the level of automation with the requirement for human validation and oversight. While our AI solution can provide high-quality recommendations or suggestions, it is essential to ensure that hospital staff remains actively involved in the decision-making process and has the final say in all clinical or medico-administrative decisions.

By leveraging our RAG capabilities, iteratively refining the model, and designing the solution as an assistance tool, we believe that Faber IA can propose a suitable AI solution that meets the requirements for explainability, transparency, and human oversight while providing valuable support to hospital staff.
